# `core/fs`'s appending, renaming and syncing half, against a real filesystem.
#
# `buri run` is the one command that hands a program authority, so it is the
# only place a checked-in case can reach `host.fs` at all — everything before
# `run` in the pipeline is hermetic, and `fs()` is what a `test` block sees.
#
# The claims, in the order the program makes them:
#
#   * `makeDir` is idempotent, so a WAL opens its own directory on every start
#     with one call rather than a check and a race;
#   * `append` creates the file and adds to its end, so two records read back
#     as four octets in the order they were written;
#   * `sync` answers on a file and on a directory, which is what makes the
#     commit point and the checkpoint swap durable rather than merely visible;
#   * `rename` replaces the destination and leaves nothing at the source;
#   * `remove` deletes, and says `NotFound` the second time rather than
#     answering `.Ok` to a path that names nothing.
#
# The same sequence runs against the `fs()` double in
# `conformance/lib/semantics/test/effects.buri` and against the native runtime
# in `cli/tests/native/runtime.rs`. Three implementations of one story is the
# point: a divergence is a failure in exactly one of them.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "the appending, renaming and syncing half of core/fs commits a write-ahead log against a real filesystem"

run {
  args: ["run", "//cmd/wal"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "wal.txt"
}
